@display("Layers")
layers: section {
  | A spec says three things about a technical product, and keeps them apart: why it exists
  | and for whom, what must hold of it as seen from outside, and the exact surface a consumer
  | relies on. Each is a [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer), and every core kind of
  | `std/spec` belongs to one. How the product gets built and delivered is no [layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer) of the spec;
  | it belongs to the implementation's project, with `std/project`.
  |
  | | [Layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer)           | Says                                           | Core kinds                                                     |
  | | --------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | | `INTENT`        | why the product exists, who it's for, what for | `goal`, `actor`, `persona`, `nongoal`, `assumption`, `quality`  |
  | | `SPECIFICATION` | what must hold, from outside, in domain words  | `rule`, `constraint`, `part`, `term`, `scenario`, `usecase`     |
  | | `CONTRACT`      | the exact technical contract                   | `op` and `api`, `command`, `screen`, `error`, `example`, types  |
  |
  | References run up or across: a lower node names the higher node it exists for, and the docs
  | walk them in reverse to show, under each node, the nodes that reference it, by their kind.

  layer: term {{
    One of `std/schema`'s `Layer` values, `INTENT`, `SPECIFICATION` or `CONTRACT`, in that
    order from top to bottom. A kind declares its layer with `kind(layer = Layer.CONTRACT)`,
    and a node is in its kind's layer. A node of a kind with no layer is outside the layer
    checks.
  }}

  extends_kind: term {{
    The core kind a custom kind names with `kind(extends = rule)`: the custom kind takes its
    parameters, fields and body, its [layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer) unless it declares one, the kinds its references may
    name unless it declares its own, its renderer, and its place in the
    [coverage](#heron.language.specifications.traceability.coverage) checks, as if its nodes were of the core kind.
  }}

  layer_reference: term {{
    A reference a node in a [layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer) makes in its own text, arguments or annotations: a link, a
    [short link](#heron.language.markup.links.short_link), a `<ref>` element, a reference-typed argument, a
    [hard reference](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference) or a
    [soft reference](#language.specifications.layers.references.soft_reference). References
    made by its children are theirs, apart from its strength, conditions and rationale, which
    are its own text.
  }}

  derived_list: term {{
    A list the docs compute under a node by walking references to it in reverse: the nodes
    of one kind whose [hard references](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference)
    name it, the errors whose prose links it among them, labelled by the kind, as "Ops" or
    "Commands", or the nodes whose `@see` names it, labelled "See also". The rules whose
    subject a node is aren't listed under it: each rule's sentence names its subject.
  }}

  @display("References never point down")
  references_point_up: rule(system.checker) {
    if {{ a node's [layer reference](#language.specifications.layers.layer_reference) names a node in a lower [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer) than its own }}
    must {{ report the reference as an error at it }}
    then {{ a contract entry may name a rule or a goal, and a rule a goal, while no goal names a rule and no rule names an error or an op }}
    rationale {{ A higher [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer) stays true while the [layers](#language.specifications.layers.layer) below it change, so it never depends on them. }}
  }

  same_layer_free: rule(system.checker) {
    mustNot {{ report a reference between two nodes of one [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer), or from or to a node of a kind with no [layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer) }}
  }

  layer_declared: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ read a kind's [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer) from its `layer` argument, or else from the kind it extends, and give a kind with neither no [layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer) }}
  }

  extends_inherits: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ give a kind that extends another its parameters, fields, body and [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer), and read its nodes as nodes of the extended kind wherever a link's target or a [coverage](#heron.language.specifications.traceability.coverage) check names that kind }}
  }

  extends_rendered: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ render a node of a kind that extends a core kind with the core kind's renderer, unless a renderer is registered for its own kind }}
  }

  links_on_lower_node: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ store every reference on the lower or later node, as an argument, an annotation or a link of that node, and accept none in the other direction }}
    then {{ adding a contract entry never edits the rule it implements }}
  }

  derived_lists_shown: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ show under each node its [derived lists](#language.specifications.layers.derived_list), one per referencing kind, labelled by the kind in the plural, with no verb, the core kinds' in the order of the contract, the specification and the intent and a custom kind's after them by name, and "See also" last, each entry a link, and mark the lists as derived from references written elsewhere }}
    example {{ under a rule: "Ops: `create_order` · Commands: `heron check` · Scenarios: `late_rejected`" }}
  }

  derived_lists_exported: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ write the [derived lists](#language.specifications.layers.derived_list) into the [agent export](#tools.docs.agents.agent_export) of each node, marked as derived }}
  }

  references: section {
    | A node references what it states a fact about. A reference is hard or soft, and each one
    | means what the kind it names makes it mean: `rule(desk, self_service)` binds the part
    | `desk` and serves the goal `self_service` because of the kinds they are.
    |
    | [Hard references](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference) are written in the constructor, as positional arguments after the
    | kind's main positional parameters: the normal way, which every example uses. `@touch`
    | writes them as an annotation, beside or instead of the constructor's, for whoever
    | prefers to split them out, and for a kind whose constructor takes none, as a type, whose
    | constructor names the interfaces it implements. The gap checks and [coverage](#heron.language.specifications.traceability.coverage) count both.
    | `@see` writes the rare [soft reference](#language.specifications.layers.references.soft_reference), for documentation: the docs show it as "See also"
    | and [coverage](#heron.language.specifications.traceability.coverage) ignores it. Prose links come first; `@see` is for a reference the prose
    | can't carry.
    |
    | A [term](#heron.language.specifications.context.term_kind) is vocabulary: a node
    | mentions it in its prose and never references it, and a mention counts for the
    | glossary, the term's "used in" and the unlinked-term lint, never for the reference
    | graph. A rule is a leaf: it states one fact about its subject, the goals and qualities
    | it serves and the assumptions it relies on, and never references another rule, in its
    | signature or its prose. An error takes no reference: it's a result, not a surface, so
    | its prose links the rule that says when it's reported, and it never names who reports
    | it. `proves`, the reference from a test, lives in the implementation's project. The
    | annotations are core, with no import.
    |
    | | Constructor                                   | Its references name                                                    | Meaning                                          |
    | | --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
    | | `rule(subject?, …)`                           | at most one actor or part; goals and qualities; assumptions           | its subject; what it serves; what it relies on   |
    | | `constraint(…, reason = …)`                   | goals and qualities; assumptions; actors and personas                 | what it serves; what it relies on; who asked     |
    | | `usecase(actor, …)`                           | goals and qualities; assumptions                                       | what it serves; what it relies on                |
    | | `goal(…)`                                     | actors and personas                                                    | who asked for it                                 |
    | | `scenario(…)`, an outcome scenario            | one rule, use case, step or extension                                  | the case it describes                            |
    | | `scenario(…)`, a quality scenario             | the quality                                                            | what it measures                                 |
    | | `example(…)`                                  | one outcome scenario                                                   | the case it pins down                            |
    | | `op(result?, …)`, `command(name, …)`, `screen(route?, …)`, `api(…)`, `region(…)`, `argument(name, …)`, `option(name, …)`, `grammar(…)`, `production(…)`, `token(…)` | the kinds in `types.realized`: rules, constraints, use cases, steps, extensions, parts, [lifecycles](#heron.language.specifications.lifecycles.lifecycle) and transitions | what it carries out; a transition, what an op performs |
    | | `@touch(…)` on a type, an enum, a union or an interface | the kinds in `types.realized`                                | what it carries out                              |
    | | `@see(…)` on any node                         | any node but a term                                                    | what it's about                                  |
    | | `proves(…)`, in a project                     | any requirement, at a [revision](#heron.language.specifications.layers.revisions.revision) | what it proves                                   |

    hard_reference: term {{
      A reference [coverage](#heron.language.specifications.traceability.coverage) and the gap checks count: a positional argument a kind's variadic
      reference parameter takes, written after its main positional parameters, or an argument
      of `@touch`, which means the same.
    }}

    soft_reference: term {{
      An argument of `@see`: a reference for documentation, which the docs show as "See also"
      and [coverage](#heron.language.specifications.traceability.coverage) and the gap checks don't count.
    }}

    reference_kind_list: term {{
      A kind's `references`, `kind(references = [actor, part, goal])`: the kinds, and the
      categories of kinds, its [hard references](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference)
      may name. A kind that extends another takes its list unless it declares its own, so a
      custom kind can widen or narrow what its nodes reference. `std/types` declares
      `realized`, the category of the spec kinds a contract surface carries out, and
      `std/spec`'s `rule`, `constraint`, `usecase`, `part`, `states` and `transition` join it.
    }}

    reference_kinds: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ a [hard reference](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference) names a node whose kind is none of its kind's [reference kind list](#language.specifications.layers.references.reference_kind_list), nor in a category on it, nor a kind that extends one }}
      must {{ report the reference as an error at it, naming the kinds it takes }}
      example {{ `rule(checker_rule)` where `checker_rule` is a rule reports that a rule references only an actor, a part, a goal, a quality or an assumption }}
    }

    op_result_first: rule(system.checker) {
      must {{ read an op's first positional argument as its [result type](#heron.language.apis.responses.result_type) when it names a type, and as its first reference otherwise, and accept the type by name too, as `returns = Order` }}
      example {{ `op(Order, place_order.3)` returns an `Order` and carries out step 3 of `place_order`; `op(place_order.3)` returns nothing }}
    }

    term_not_referenced: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ a [hard](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference) or [soft reference](#language.specifications.layers.references.soft_reference) names a [term](#heron.language.specifications.context.term_kind) }}
      must {{ report it as an error at the reference, saying to mention the term in the prose }}
    }

    one_subject: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ a rule's [hard references](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference) name more than one actor, persona or part }}
      must {{ report the second as an error at it }}
      then {{ a rule binds one subject, and a rule that references none binds the system }}
    }

    no_rule_to_rule: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ a rule's strength, conditions, rationale or `@see` link another rule }}
      must {{ report the link as an error at it }}
      then {{ a rule states its own fact and links the term, part or section it shares with another, while an error or a scenario below it may link it }}
    }

    references_acyclic: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ the references of the layered nodes, in their constructors, annotations and prose, the [terms](#heron.language.specifications.context.term_kind) they mention left out, come back to a node }}
      must {{ report the cycle as an error at the reference that closes it }}
      then {{ with every reference pointing up or across, the references form a graph with no cycle }}
    }

    touch_annotation: rule(system.checker) {
      must {{ accept `@touch(targets…)` on a node of any kind and read its targets as [hard references](#language.specifications.layers.references.hard_reference) of the node, checked against its kind's [reference kind list](#language.specifications.layers.references.reference_kind_list), and on a kind with no list against the kinds in `types.realized`, reading a target written `usecase.2` or `usecase.2a` as that step or extension of the use case }}
      example {{ `@touch(refund_window) Refund: type { … }` and `@touch(refund_window) create(): op(Refund)` are accepted; `@touch(self_service)` above a type is reported }}
    }

    listed_twice: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ a node names one target both in its constructor and in its `@touch` }}
      must {{ report the `@touch` target as an error at it, saying it's listed twice }}
    }

    see_annotation: rule(system.checker) {
      must {{ accept `@see(targets…)` on a node of any kind, naming any node but a term, and check its targets for direction and cycles as any reference, while [coverage](#heron.language.specifications.traceability.coverage) and the gap checks leave them out }}
    }

    area_goals: rule(system.checker) {
      where {{ a rule, a constraint or a use case references no goal or quality }}
      must {{ read it as serving the goals declared in the nearest section above it that declares any, the package's own included }}
      then {{ an area that opens with its goals motivates the requirements in it, and a requirement references a goal of its own only where it serves another }}
    }
  }

  revisions: section {
    | A requirement's meaning changes rarely and on purpose. Its `@rev` says which meaning it has,
    | and a proof names the [revision](#heron.language.specifications.layers.revisions.revision) it was written against, so a changed requirement shows which
    | of its proofs need a second look.

    revision: term {{
      A requirement's `@rev(n)`, a whole number from 1, the default: on a rule, a constraint,
      a scenario, a use case, a contract entry or an error.
    }}

    revision_bump: rule(actors.author) {
      when {{ a change alters what a requirement means, so a proof of the old meaning may no longer prove it }}
      must {{ raise its [revision](#language.specifications.layers.revisions.revision) by one in the same change }}
      rationale {{ A wording fix keeps the [revision](#language.specifications.layers.revisions.revision); a change of behaviour doesn't. }}
    }

    revision_positive: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ a `@rev` is less than 1 }}
      must {{ report it as an error at the value }}
    }
  }

  @display("A goal, a rule and the op that implements it")
  layers_example: example {
    | ```heron
    | import = ["std/doc@1", "std/types@1", "std/apis@1", "std/http@1"]
    |
    | self_service: goal(measure = "Most refunds need no support ticket.") {{
    |   Customers fix a wrong charge themselves.
    | }}
    |
    | customer: actor {{ Someone who paid with the shop. }}
    |
    | desk: part {{ The support console. }}
    |
    | refund_in_window: rule(desk, self_service) {
    |   when {{ a [customer] asks for a refund inside the refund window }}
    |   must {{ refund the amount asked for }}
    | }
    |
    | payments: api {
    |   @http<post>("/refunds")
    |   create_refund(amount: Int): op(Int, refund_in_window)
    | }
    | ```
  }

  errors: section {
    @display("Reference points down")
    points_down: error("layer") {
      title = "{name} is a {kind} in the {to} layer, and a {from} node can't reference a lower layer; link it from the lower node instead"
      name: field(String)
      kind: field(String)
      to: field(String)
      from: field(String)
      | A goal linking a rule, or a rule linking an op or an error. The fix is a reference on the lower node: a reference in its constructor or `@touch`, or a link to the rule in the error's prose.
      | Reported as [layers.references_point_up] says.
    }

    @display("Reference to a kind not taken")
    wrong_reference: error("reference") {
      title = "{name} is a {kind}, and a {from} references only {kinds}"
      name: field(String)
      kind: field(String)
      from: field(String)
      kinds: field(String)
      | Reported as [layers.references.reference_kinds] says, and on a kind with no list as "{name} is a {kind}, and @touch on a {from} takes only a rule, a constraint, a use case or its step, a part, a [lifecycle](#heron.language.specifications.lifecycles.lifecycle) or a transition", as [layers.references.touch_annotation] says. A term gets its own message, "{name} is a term, which is vocabulary: a {from} doesn't reference it; mention it in the prose", or "@see doesn't reference it" for a [soft reference](#heron.language.specifications.layers.references.soft_reference), as [layers.references.term_not_referenced] says.
    }

    @display("Two subjects")
    two_subjects: error("subject") {
      title = "rule {name} binds {first} and {second}; a rule references at most one actor or part, its subject"
      name: field(String)
      first: field(String)
      second: field(String)
      | Reported as [layers.references.one_subject] says.
    }

    @display("Rule linking a rule")
    rule_links_rule: error("rule-link") {
      title = "rule {name} links the rule {target}; a rule references no other rule, so link the term, part or section they share, or state the fact in the rule itself"
      name: field(String)
      target: field(String)
      | Reported as [layers.references.no_rule_to_rule] says.
    }

    @display("Reference listed twice")
    listed_twice: error("reference") {
      title = "{name} is listed twice, in the constructor and in @touch; list it once"
      name: field(String)
      | Reported as [layers.references.listed_twice] says.
    }

    @display("Reference cycle")
    reference_cycle: error("cycle") {
      title = "{name} references itself through {path}"
      name: field(String)
      path: field(String)
      | Reported as [layers.references.references_acyclic] says.
    }

    @display("Revision below 1")
    revision_below_one: error("type") {
      title = "a revision is a whole number from 1, not {value}"
      value: field(String)
      | Reported as [layers.revisions.revision_positive] says.
    }
  }
}
